research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-second-mean-value-theorem-for-integrals.md-4597-[L1] $F(y) - F(x) = \int_x^y f$ for all $x,y \in [a,b]$, in either order ([[def-the-integral-function]]).
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-second-mean-value-theorem-for-integrals.md-4598-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-second-mean-value-theorem-for-integrals.md-4599-[L2] $|f|$ is integrable on every $[p,q] \subseteq [a,b]$ with $p<q$, and $\bigl|\int_p^q f\bigr| \le \int_p^q |f|$ there ([[cor-integrability-of-absolute-values-products-and-lattice-operations]], claims 1 and 3).
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-second-mean-value-theorem-for-integrals.md-4600-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-second-mean-value-theorem-for-integrals.md:4601:[L3] If $u \le w$ pointwise on $[p,q]$ and both are integrable then $\int_p^q u \le \int_p^q w$, and $\int_p^q c = c(q-p)$ for a constant $c$ ([[thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral]], [[lem-integral-elementary-bounds]]).
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-second-mean-value-theorem-for-integrals.md-4602-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-second-mean-value-theorem-for-integrals.md-4603-[L4] With oriented limits, $\int_y^x f = -\int_x^y f$ and $\int_x^x f = 0$ ([[def-oriented-integral]]).
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-second-mean-value-theorem-for-integrals.md-4604-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-second-mean-value-theorem-for-integrals.md-4605-[L5] Absolute value: $|{-t}| = |t|$, $|t| \ge 0$, and $|t| \le c$ follows from $-c \le t \le c$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]], [[def-abs-value]]).
