research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-24-{"id":"ex-positive-derivative-at-zero-with-no-local-monotonicity","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"trivial","urls":[],"rationale":"The exact witness x+x^2 psi(1/x) is a local composition with the bespoke oscillator. Its derivative at zero is 1 by direct division, while the recurring values psi'=3 and -3 force derivative values of opposite sign arbitrarily close to zero; the mean value theorem then rules out monotonicity on every neighbourhood.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-25-{"id":"ex-lhopital-rational-removable-quotient","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"trivial","urls":[],"rationale":"This particular removable rational quotient is a locally selected worked example. Factoring x^3-x=x(x-1)(x+1) and x^2-1=(x-1)(x+1) reduces the quotient to x off 1, and the derivative quotient independently tends to 1, so the claim is directly verified.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-26-{"id":"cex-lhopital-converse","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"trivial","urls":[],"rationale":"The exact counterexample is built from the bespoke periodic polynomial oscillator. With f(x)=x^2 psi(1/x) and g(x)=x, the quotient tends to zero by boundedness, but f'/g'=2x psi(1/x)-psi'(1/x) has two recurring subsequential limits. This directly refutes the converse without a literature-dependent step.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-27-{"id":"ex-geometric-function-taylor-remainder","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_taylor.html"],"rationale":"The source develops the Taylor polynomials for the geometric rational function and the geometric-series coefficients. The item records the finite exact algebraic remainder x^{n+1}/(1-x), valid for every x not equal to 1, rather than using a convergence claim.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-28-{"id":"ex-zero-second-derivative-extremum-trichotomy","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_taylor.html","https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~voronov/5615-20/pdfs/12-2-1.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give the inconclusive zero-second-derivative warning and the higher-derivative test. The item packages the canonical x^4, -x^4, and x^3 witnesses and checks their derivatives and local order directly.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-29-{"id":"def-oriented-integral","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Integral_calculus"],"rationale":"The source states the same orientation convention: reversing the endpoints negates the integral and an equal-endpoint integral is zero. The item only binds that standard convention to the library's Darboux integral.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-30-{"id":"lem-integrability-on-a-subinterval","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_rintprop.html"],"rationale":"The source states that Riemann integrability on [a,b] implies integrability on every closed subinterval, with the same hypotheses and direction. The Darboux-criterion proof in the item is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-31-{"id":"thm-linearity-of-the-integral","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Integral_calculus"],"rationale":"The sources state closure under scalar multiplication and addition and the identical linearity identity. The repository supplies a local upper/lower-sum proof. Its statement is correct; the separate step-reference typo in proof step 4.2 is recorded in the findings.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl:32:{"id":"thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Integral_calculus"],"rationale":"The sources state that pointwise f<=g for two Riemann-integrable functions implies the same order between their integrals. The item extends it to oriented notation by a direct local case split.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-33-{"id":"thm-additivity-over-subintervals","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_rintprop.html","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Integral_calculus"],"rationale":"The sources give additivity for an ordered interior split and the subinterval integrability equivalence. The item packages the result for every third point and every endpoint order using the oriented-integral convention.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-34-{"id":"lem-changing-a-function-at-finitely-many-points","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_rintprop.html"],"rationale":"The source states that changing a bounded function at finitely many points preserves Riemann integrability and the integral. The item's oscillation-budget proof is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-35-{"id":"thm-composition-with-a-continuous-function","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00591-022-00318-x"],"rationale":"The peer-reviewed article states the same preservation theorem for a Riemann-integrable inner function and a continuous outer function on an interval containing its range. The item's uniform-continuity proof is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-36-{"id":"cor-integrability-of-absolute-values-products-and-lattice-operations","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Integral_calculus"],"rationale":"The sources give closure of Riemann-integrable functions under absolute value, products, maxima and minima. The item packages all four operations as one corollary and obtains the lattice formulas through continuous composition and algebraic identities.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-37-{"id":"thm-first-mean-value-theorem-for-integrals","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Integral_calculus"],"rationale":"The source states the broader weighted first mean value theorem for a one-signed integrable weight and places the witness in the open interval. The published item specializes to g >= 0, permits an endpoint witness, and gives a local proof with explicit zero-weight handling, so the source is semantic rather than verbatim support.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-10"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-38-{"id":"def-the-integral-function","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Integral_calculus","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The sources define the variable-upper-limit integral function F(x)=integral_a^x f. The item merely fixes the same object in the library's oriented-integral notation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-39-{"id":"thm-the-integral-function-is-lipschitz","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The source proves that the integral function of a bounded Riemann-integrable function satisfies |F(x)-F(y)|<=M|x-y| and is continuous. The item's proof is the same mathematical estimate but was written locally from its integral bounds.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-40-{"id":"thm-ftc-first-part","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The sources state that the integral function of a Riemann-integrable f is differentiable at every point where f is continuous, with derivative f at that point. The repository adds explicit one-sided endpoint language already fixed by its derivative definition.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
